<ArticleLayout slug="bc-pnp-veterinarians-vet-technologists-2027" datePublished="2026-10-14" dateModified="2026-10-14" articleSection="Occupation Guide" keywords="BC PNP veterinarian, BC PNP veterinary technologist, NOC 31103, NOC 32104, CVBC licensing, NEB certificate of qualification, RVT BCVTA, BC PNP Care draws 2027" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-emerald-200 shadow-sm">
        Occupation Guide &bull; Care Priority
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP for Veterinarians and Vet Technologists in 2027: Licensing, Draws and Strategy
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        Veterinary care is one of the smallest but most consistent BC PNP draw categories. Here is how licensing, registration and the points grid fit together for NOC 31103 and NOC 32104.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-600 mb-2">142</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Vets (26) and vet technologists (116) nominated since 2022</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">72&ndash;100</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Minimum scores in 2026 Veterinary Care draws</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-amber-600 mb-2">25</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Veterinary care nominations in 2025</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        When the BC Provincial Nominee Program reorganized itself on April 23, 2026 around three objectives &mdash; Care, Build and Innovate &mdash; veterinary care was named alongside healthcare, education and childcare as part of the "care economy." The program's own wording is specific: veterinarians and veterinary technologists <strong>who are working toward Canadian certification</strong> will be prioritized. That phrase matters, because it means you do not always need a finished Canadian licence before you can compete. It also means your licensing progress is the single biggest factor in whether you are a credible candidate.
      </p>

      <p>
        This guide covers the two eligible occupations, what the 2026 draws actually looked like, the licensing routes run by the College of Veterinarians of British Columbia (CVBC), the National Examining Board (NEB) and the BC Veterinary Technologists Association (BCVTA), and how to plan for 2027 when the next set of priorities has not yet been published.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Two Eligible Occupations</h2>

      <p>
        The BC PNP's "Selection of workers" list on WelcomeBC places two NOC codes under <strong>Care: Veterinary care</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">NOC</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Occupation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">TEER</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Minimum language for BC PNP</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">BC credential that matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">31103</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Veterinarians</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">1</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">No fixed minimum at registration (test optional unless claiming points or requested)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">CVBC registration (requires an NEB Certificate of Qualification for full practice)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">32104</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Animal health technologists and veterinary technicians</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">2</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 4 in all four skills</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Registered Veterinary Technologist (RVT) with the BCVTA</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The language rules come from section 3.4 of the Skills Immigration Program Guide: job offers in TEER 2 to 5 need CLB 4 in each skill, while TEER 0 and 1 job offers do not need a test unless you claim language points or the BC PNP asks for one. In practice almost every veterinarian should still test, because language is worth up to 40 of the 200 registration points.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both codes also appear on the Health Authority stream's list of eligible occupations, but that stream requires a regular, indeterminate, full-time job as a <strong>direct employee</strong> of a B.C. public health authority. Very few veterinary roles fit that description, so for most people the route is the Skilled Worker stream (or Express Entry BC) with a private clinic, hospital or other eligible employer, competing in the Veterinary Care draws. For how the Care groups compare, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-care-healthcare-occupations-list-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Care occupations list for 2027</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the 2026 Veterinary Care Draws Looked Like</h2>

      <p>
        Draws under the new Care and Build labels began on May 6, 2026. WelcomeBC's invitations page shows six Veterinary Care rounds up to October 8, 2026:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Draw date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Minimum score</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Invitations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">May 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">June 2, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">92</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">July 9, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88</td><td class="px-4 py-3">&lt;5</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">August 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">September 10, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">90</td><td class="px-4 py-3">&lt;5</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">October 8, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">86</td><td class="px-4 py-3">&lt;5</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Three things stand out. First, the draws are <strong>tiny</strong> &mdash; usually single digits &mdash; which fits the BC PNP Statistical Report 2025, where veterinary care nominations were 41 in 2023, 48 in 2024 and 25 in 2025. Second, the cutoffs are <strong>far lower</strong> than the points-based High Economic Impact draws, which needed 131 to 136 points between May and September 2026. Third, the cutoff <strong>moves around</strong>: it fell to 72 in August and climbed back to 90 the next month. With so few candidates, one or two high-scoring registrations can move the line.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each Veterinary Care row on WelcomeBC lists two selection factors under one score and one invitation count: "All priority veterinary care occupations" and "Animal health technologists and veterinary technicians (NOC 32104) with valid professional designation." The table does not spell out how the two interact, but the clear signal is that, for vet technologists, a valid B.C. professional designation is part of what gets you selected.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Key takeaway</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">In 2026, a veterinary candidate scoring in the low 90s would have been invited in most Veterinary Care draws, but you could not count on any single round. The bigger hurdle is usually licensing and a qualifying job offer, not the score.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Veterinarians: The CVBC and NEB Licensing Path</h2>

      <p>
        In British Columbia, veterinarians are regulated by the CVBC. Its registration guide lists the core requirements: a degree in veterinary medicine, occupational competency, English language proficiency, status to work in Canada, proof of good character and fitness to practise, and the CVBC Bylaw and Ethics Exam. An application stays open for one year from activation; if it stalls for a year it is closed and you must start again with new forms and fees.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: The Certificate of Qualification (CQ)</h3>

      <p>
        The CVBC says a CQ from the NEB (run by the Canadian Veterinary Medical Association) is required for full Private Practice registration or Temporary (Private Practice) registration. Your route to the CQ depends on where you studied. Graduates of AVMA/CVMA-accredited schools follow one path; graduates of non-accredited schools must complete the NEB's assessment process. The NEB currently describes four options for non-accredited graduates:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">NEB pathway</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">What it involves</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Watch out for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Regular NEB process</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Registration and credential assessment, then the BCSE, NAVLE and Clinical Proficiency Examination (CPE)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Pathways are not interchangeable; on this route you take every exam</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">ECFVG transfer</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Transfer BCSE and CPE results from the AVMA's ECFVG program; NAVLE via AAVSB or NEB</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">An ECFVG certificate earned through the clinical year option is not recognized</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">NEB + clinical year</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">BCSE, then a final clinical year at a CVMA/AVMA-accredited school, then the NAVLE</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Only for those who have passed the BCSE; you arrange the clinical year yourself; CPE needed if NAVLE is not passed within three attempts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">NEB + PAVE</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">AAVSB's PAVE (QSE plus evaluated clinical experience), then the NAVLE</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">Only if you passed the QSE since September 2014; CPE needed if NAVLE is not passed within three attempts</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Provisional Supervised Active (PSA) registration</h3>

      <p>
        PSA registration is the CVBC's bridge for people who are part-way through the NEB process. According to the CVBC, you may qualify if you trained at a non-accredited school, are an NEB candidate and have passed the written exams but not yet the practical exams &mdash; or, if you trained at an accredited school, needed more than three NAVLE attempts and still have practical exams to complete. PSA registrants practise only under supervision. The CVBC's 2024&ndash;2025 Annual Report says its Registration Committee approved 35 PSA applications that year, and 23 PSA registrants received their CQ and moved to full Private Practice registration.
      </p>

      <p>
        For BC PNP purposes, PSA registration is valuable because it is concrete evidence that you are "working toward Canadian certification." Section 3.7 of the program guide says that if a job requires mandatory licensing, you must either show you meet it at application or "outline the steps you have taken to show that these requirements will shortly be met." A PSA approval, exam results and a supervision agreement tell that story far better than a plan to start the NEB process later.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Common mistake</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">Registering with a veterinarian job offer before you have any NEB progress. If you are invited and cannot show credible steps toward licensing, the application can be refused &mdash; and the $1,750 application fee is only refunded if you withdraw before assessment begins.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Vet Technologists: Getting RVT Status Through the BCVTA</h2>

      <p>
        "Registered Veterinary Technologist" is a protected title in B.C. The BCVTA lists three requirements: graduate from an accredited veterinary technology program, pass the Veterinary Technician National Exam (VTNE), and register with the BCVTA. Members must then complete at least 15 hours of continuing education each year. (Older CVBC material from 2022 says 10 hours; the BCVTA's current page says 15.)
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Trained in B.C.:</strong> the BCVTA lists Thompson Rivers University (on-campus and Open Learning diplomas) and Douglas College as recognized programs.</li>
        <li><strong>RVT elsewhere in North America:</strong> if you are an active member of your current licensing body and meet BCVTA eligibility, you can keep RVT status; your current body sends a Letter of Good Standing.</li>
        <li><strong>Trained outside North America:</strong> the BCVTA uses the International Credential Assessment Service of Canada (ICAS) to assess your education. The BCVTA board then reviews the assessment and may require extra courses or training. Once you meet its criteria, you are approved to write the VTNE, and passing it lets you complete registration.</li>
        <li><strong>Veterinarians:</strong> the BCVTA does not currently accept membership applications from veterinarians.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The VTNE is owned and administered by the American Association of Veterinary State Boards and is offered in three exam windows each year, so build that calendar into your timeline. Once registered, you earn <strong>5 professional designation points</strong> in the BC PNP education factor &mdash; the program guide lists "Veterinary Technicians registered with British Columbia Veterinary Technologists Association" as an eligible designation for NOC 32104.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Worked Examples: How Veterinary Candidates Score</h2>

      <p>
        The examples below use the point tables in Part 8 of the current Skills Immigration Program Guide. They are illustrations, not predictions; plug your own numbers into our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example A: Internationally trained RVT in Kamloops</h3>

      <p>
        Priya trained abroad, completed the ICAS assessment and the VTNE, and is now an RVT. She has 3 years of directly related experience, including 14 months full-time with her Kamloops clinic, which is supporting her. Her diploma was earned outside Canada, her lowest CLB score is 7, and her job offer pays $26.00 an hour.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Factor</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Basis</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Points</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Work experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3 years (12) + 1 year in Canada (10) + currently working for the employer (10)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">32</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Post-secondary diploma (5) + BCVTA professional designation (5)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Language</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7</td><td class="px-4 py-3">20</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Wage</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$26.00&ndash;$26.99 an hour</td><td class="px-4 py-3">11</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Area</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Area 3 (15) + one year of regional experience (10)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3"></td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">98</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        At 98, Priya would have cleared five of the six 2026 Veterinary Care cutoffs. Without RVT status she would lose the 5 designation points &mdash; and, more importantly, possibly the selection factor itself.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example B: Foreign-trained veterinarian on PSA registration in Vernon</h3>

      <p>
        Daniel has 6 years of experience as a veterinarian, the last 12 months full-time in a Vernon mixed practice under CVBC PSA registration while he completes the NEB practical exams. For this illustration, assume his veterinary degree is credited at bachelor's level (15 points); the BC PNP decides how your credential is scored. His lowest CLB score is 8 and his job offer pays $40.00 an hour.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Factor</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Basis</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Points</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Work experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5+ years (20) + 1 year in Canada (10) + currently working (10)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">40</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Bachelor's level (15); veterinarians are not on the designation-points list</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Language</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 8</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Wage</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$40.00&ndash;$40.99 an hour</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Area</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Area 3 (15) + regional experience (10)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3"></td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">130</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Daniel's score clears every 2026 Veterinary Care cutoff with room to spare. His risk is not the score; it is showing the BC PNP that full licensing will "shortly be met."
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Wages and Where the Jobs Are</h2>

      <p>
        The BC PNP requires your wage to sit within the WorkBC or Job Bank range for the occupation and location. WorkBC's career profiles give the following provincial figures (derived from 2025 Job Bank wage data) and 10-year job openings from the B.C. Labour Market Outlook:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Measure</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Veterinarians (31103)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Vet technologists (32104)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Hourly wage: low / median / high</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$35.58 / $60.00 / $86.54</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$17.85 / $23.00 / $33.17</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Job openings 2025&ndash;2035</td><td class="px-4 py-3">790</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,160</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Openings: Mainland/Southwest</td><td class="px-4 py-3">370</td><td class="px-4 py-3">500</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Openings: Vancouver Island/Coast</td><td class="px-4 py-3">280</td><td class="px-4 py-3">400</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Openings: Thompson-Okanagan</td><td class="px-4 py-3">120</td><td class="px-4 py-3">230</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two practical points follow. A vet technologist at the provincial median wage earns only 8 wage points, so regional points (up to 25) and the designation bonus carry much more weight than they do for veterinarians. And outside Metro Vancouver, the Island and the Thompson-Okanagan have by far the most projected openings. WelcomeBC notes that many of the 142 veterinary nominees since 2022 work outside Metro Vancouver. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-look-west-regional-nominations-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">guide to regional nominations under Look West</a> explains how area points work.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Express Entry as a Parallel Route</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC's category-based selection page lists both NOC 31103 and 32104 in the <strong>Healthcare and social services</strong> category for 2026. To qualify you need at least 12 months of full-time work experience (or the part-time equivalent) in an eligible occupation within the past 3 years, plus eligibility for an Express Entry program. If you qualify for Express Entry, using the Express Entry BC option for your BC PNP application keeps both doors open. IRCC consulted on its 2027 categories in summer 2026 and has not announced the result; see our <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry 2027 category guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 2027 Outlook (Analysis)</h2>

      <p>
        Nothing about 2027 has been announced yet. B.C. has not received a 2027 nomination allocation, and the federal 2027&ndash;2029 Immigration Levels Plan is expected in early November 2026. With that caveat, here is what we expect if current patterns hold:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Veterinary care likely stays in Care.</strong> B.C. has prioritized vets and registered vet technologists since 2022, and the April 2026 restructuring kept them. We see no sign of that changing, but priorities are reviewed and can be revised at any time.</li>
        <li><strong>Draws should stay small.</strong> Nominations in this group have ranged from 25 to 48 a year. Expect single-digit invitations per round rather than large rounds.</li>
        <li><strong>Cutoffs will keep swinging.</strong> The 2026 range was 72 to 100. In a pool this small, we would not try to predict a number &mdash; a score in the 90s has been competitive, but a single strong registrant can lift any round.</li>
        <li><strong>Licensing progress will matter more than points.</strong> The "working toward Canadian certification" wording rewards people who can document real steps: NEB candidacy, exam results, PSA registration, or BCVTA membership.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Who This Pathway Suits &mdash; and Who It Does Not</h2>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
        <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 p-6 rounded-2xl">
          <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-2">Good fit</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-green-800 space-y-2 text-base">
            <li>RVTs (or vet techs close to passing the VTNE) with a B.C. clinic willing to support them</li>
            <li>Veterinarians with NEB progress and PSA or full CVBC registration</li>
            <li>Candidates open to working on Vancouver Island or in the Interior</li>
            <li>People who also qualify for Express Entry's healthcare category</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 p-6 rounded-2xl">
          <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-2">Poor fit (for now)</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-red-800 space-y-2 text-base">
            <li>Veterinarians who have not started the NEB process</li>
            <li>Veterinary assistants or kennel staff whose duties do not match NOC 32104</li>
            <li>Foreign veterinarians hoping to use RVT status as a stepping stone (the BCVTA does not accept veterinarians)</li>
            <li>Anyone without a job offer from an eligible B.C. employer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Step-by-Step Plan for 2027</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Lock in your credential path.</strong> Veterinarians: open an NEB file and identify your pathway. Vet technologists trained outside North America: start the ICAS assessment through the BCVTA.</li>
        <li><strong>Book exams around the windows.</strong> The VTNE runs in three windows a year, so missing one can cost months. Check the NEB candidate portal for current exam dates before you commit to a timeline.</li>
        <li><strong>Secure an eligible employer.</strong> The employer must have operated in B.C. for at least one year and have at least five full-time employees in Metro Vancouver, or three outside it.</li>
        <li><strong>Take an approved language test.</strong> CELPIP-General, IELTS General Training, PTE Core, TEF Canada or TCF Canada. Results are valid for two years.</li>
        <li><strong>Register when you can show progress.</strong> Registration is free. Your registration is verified later, so claim only what you can prove.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep your status valid.</strong> The BC PNP will not nominate anyone who is out of status. Plan work permit renewals early; see our guide to <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">work permit holders and the temporary resident cap</a>.</li>
      </ol>

